<Docs
  title="Troubleshooting"
  description="Fix the common setup, adapter, preview, worktree and terminal problems without guessing."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Start with the app">
    The app reports the command, adapter tier, working directory and failure category. Open the
    task's diagnostics before changing configuration; the site cannot see your installed versions.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="agents">Agent not found</h2>
  <p>
    Shepherd launches an installed CLI; it does not bundle an agent. Check that the executable is on
    the path the app sees, or pin its absolute path in <code>settings.json</code> under the matching
    <code>agents.&lt;name&gt;.executable</code> key. Restart Shepherd after editing settings.
  </p>
  <p>
    For Claude Code, the ACP executable is a separate binary from the terminal CLI. Set
    <code>agents.claude.executable</code> and, if needed, <code>agents.claude.args</code>. For
    OpenCode, <code>agents.opencode.executable</code> and an optional
    <code>agents.opencode.config_file</code> are enough.
  </p>

  <h2 id="auth">Authentication</h2>
  <p>
    Authenticate in the agent's own CLI first. Shepherd stores no provider keys and cannot repair an
    expired login. If a launch environment value begins with <code>!</code>, its command must succeed
    within five seconds; a failure blocks the spawn. Shepherd hides adapter stderr when launch
    environment values may contain secrets, so check the failure category and the agent's own
    diagnostics.
  </p>

  <h2 id="fallback">Adapter fallback</h2>
  <p>
    A task can fall back to a lower adapter tier when the installed CLI does not support the richer
    protocol. The conversation header and diagnostics show the tier and the reason. A fallback is not
    automatically a failure: the agent may still stream output and accept prompts, but approvals or
    resume can be unavailable. The UI hides actions the connected adapter cannot perform rather than
    showing a dead control.
  </p>
  <p>
    If a session cannot resume, the task says so and offers a new session with transcript replay. It
    does not pretend the old session survived.
  </p>

  <h2 id="preview">Preview</h2>
  <p>
    The preview only accepts a loopback HTTP(S) URL. If the page is blank, confirm the URL in Settings
    → Projects and open it directly in a browser. Element selection needs script injection; when a
    page refuses framing or injection, or navigates off the configured origin, use the screenshot
    picker instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the external wrapper refuses to submit, do not copy the URL between browser profiles. The
    first load claims the wrapper with an HttpOnly cookie, and later POSTs require that exact browser,
    origin and task. Reopen the wrapper from the app after changing projects or tasks.
  </p>

  <h2 id="git">Worktrees and Git</h2>
  <p>
    Agent tasks require a Git repository. Shepherd creates the worktree under the resolved Git
    directory, not inside the checkout. Removing a task refuses when the worktree is dirty; commit or
    discard the changes first. Archived worktrees are collected after 30 days only when clean, and
    reopening recreates them from the branch or recorded tip.
  </p>
  <p>
    If the root is on another branch, a new task still starts from the project's default branch.
    Squash-merge is disabled on a dirty or off-base root. Git failures appear in the app's error toast
    with retry controls; a hook failure preserves the commit message.
  </p>

  <h2 id="terminals">Terminals</h2>
  <p>
    Closing a terminal view does not stop its PTY. Reopen it from the Terminals tab's instance menu.
    Quitting the app ends every PTY; the next launch shows those terminals as ended with their last
    directory and command. Use Restart to create a new shell.
  </p>
  <p>
    A shared terminal belongs to the project and appears in every task. A task terminal appears only
    while its task is selected. If focus seems stuck, remember that arrow keys go to the PTY while
    terminal input is focused; use the app's focus commands to leave it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="logs">Settings and logs</h2>
  <p>
    A malformed <code>settings.json</code> is copied to <code>.bak</code>, defaults load, and a banner
    names the path. Repair the file and use Reload settings. Keybindings have the same repair path in
    Settings → Keyboard shortcuts; a newer file version stays intact.
  </p>
  <p>
    Runtime errors appear in a toast and in Error history in the status bar. The structured log is
    <code>logs/errors.jsonl</code> in the active data directory, normally
    <code>.cache/test-db/logs/errors.jsonl</code> for a development run. Follow it with
    <code>tail -F</code>. New log files are owner-only on Unix.
  </p>
  <p>
    To isolate a problem from your normal project list and database, run <code>shep --test</code>.
    If the problem reproduces there, the fastest useful report includes the OS, agent version, adapter
    tier, the diagnostics text and the matching error record.
  </p>
</Docs>
